From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 20:43:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] engine: GhosttyControlCore, the second IControlCore
 implementation

Adds the ghostty engine behind "engine": "ghostty" (ADR 0001/0003/0006).
GhosttyEngine brings libghostty up once per process and routes its
actions back to whichever core owns the surface; GhosttyControlCore is
the IControlCore/ICoreState implementation over one libghostty surface.

WT keeps the pty. Output arrives on the connection's thread, converts
UTF-16 -> UTF-8 through a per-pane til::u16state, and goes into
ghostty_surface_write_pty_output; the terminal's input comes back through
the engine's write callback into ITerminalConnection::WriteInput. The
grid size reaches the child through resize_pty_cb -> Connection.Resize,
so libghostty's cell arithmetic is the only authority for it.

Rendering is libghostty's, into a DirectComposition swap chain bound to
the same SwapChainPanel path cascadia uses (Phase 2's proof).

Two integration bugs, both found by running it rather than reading it:

- ghostty_init needs more stack than the 1 MB MSVC gives a main thread.
  Calling it on the UI thread killed WindowsTerminal.exe with
  STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW before any pane appeared - the same failure the
  Phase 0 harness hit, which is why that links /STACK:16MB. The engine
  now bootstraps on its own thread with a 16 MB reserve, which keeps the
  requirement in the engine that has it instead of raising WT's reserve.

- TsfDataProvider::_getCore did get_self<ControlCore> unconditionally.
  That is the seventh escape ADR 0001 scoped per-engine, and it does not
  fail safe: the cast yields a NON-null garbage pointer, so the null
  check below it never fired and the first ghostty pane died in
  Renderer::GetRenderData. It now QIs for the ControlCore class
  interface. That alone was not enough either - TSF's Implementation
  dereferences the renderer the provider returns without a null check
  (Implementation.cpp:197) - so TermControl no longer registers with TSF
  at all when the engine cannot back it. A ghostty pane has no IME,
  which is where DESIGN.md already puts it.

Verified in the deployed dev package: WT launches on a ghostty profile
and stays up, with OpenConsole.exe --headless --width 56 --height 18 and
cmd.exe beneath it - a real ConPTY, sized through our resize path, with a
live shell in it. NOT verified: that the pane renders pixels, or that
typing reaches the shell. WGC cannot capture on this session and stealing
foreground to synthesize keystrokes was not safe to do unattended, so
both are left for the human evaluation step.

Known incomplete: the settings translator is a no-op (ghostty_config_t
has no C setter API yet, so a pane renders with ghostty's defaults), and
mouse/selection/search/marks return their empty answers - Phase 6 scope.

